---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faq = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the SendGrid SPF record include value?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The official SendGrid SPF mechanism is include:sendgrid.net (for example, v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com include:sendgrid.net ~all). Enabling SendGrid Domain Authentication with Automated Security (the emXXXX CNAME) authenticates SPF on that subdomain automatically without requiring include:sendgrid.net on your root domain."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does SendGrid give me CNAME records instead of TXT records for SPF and DKIM?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "By default, SendGrid enables Automated Security, which uses 3 CNAME records (emXXXX, s1._domainkey, and s2._domainkey) pointing to SendGrid's zone. This delegates SPF IP management and 2048-bit DKIM key rotation to SendGrid so your authentication never breaks during infrastructure updates."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is adding include:sendgrid.net to my root SPF record necessary if I set up the 3 SendGrid CNAMEs?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In practice, root SPF records close to the 10-DNS-lookup limit omit include:sendgrid.net and rely on the emXXXX CNAME (which costs 0 lookups on your root domain and passes DMARC alignment). Add include:sendgrid.net to your root SPF only if you disabled Automated Security."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why is SendGrid's default v=DMARC1; p=none; record missing a rua= tag?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In real-world setups, SendGrid generates a bare v=DMARC1; p=none; snippet simply to satisfy Gmail and Yahoo's minimum requirement without knowing where to send your reports. Always append rua=mailto:your-report-address@yourdomain.com; to receive daily XML visibility into who sends as your domain."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Set Up SPF, DKIM, and DMARC for SendGrid: Step-by-Step DNS Guide"
  description="Set up SPF, DKIM, and DMARC for SendGrid: complete guide to Automated Security CNAME records, include:sendgrid.net, s1 and s2 selectors, and Cloudflare DNS fixes."
  schema={[faq]}
  breadcrumb={[
    ["Home", "/"],
    ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"],
    ["SendGrid SPF, DKIM & DMARC"]
  ]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SendGrid SPF, DKIM &amp; DMARC</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      Set Up SPF, DKIM, and DMARC for SendGrid: Step-by-Step DNS Guide
    </h1>

    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        Authenticating SendGrid requires <strong class="text-ink">publishing 3 delegated CNAME records (<code>emXXXX</code>, <code>s1._domainkey</code>, and <code>s2._domainkey</code>) for Automated Security (or adding <code>include:sendgrid.net</code> to your root SPF record for manual security) and publishing a <code>_dmarc</code> TXT policy</strong>. These records replace SendGrid's shared bounce domain with yours. The setup ensures 100% DMARC alignment and inbox placement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#sendgrid-email-authentication-specification-automated-cnames-vs-manual-txt-records">SendGrid authentication specification</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-1-how-to-start-domain-authentication-in-the-sendgrid-dashboard-4-steps">Step 1: Start domain authentication</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-2-how-to-set-up-sendgrid-spf-emxxxx-cname-vs-includesendgridnet">Step 2: Set up SendGrid SPF</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-3-how-to-set-up-sendgrid-dkim-s1domainkey-and-s2domainkey-cnames">Step 3: Set up SendGrid DKIM</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-4-how-to-configure-dmarc-for-sendgrid-dmarc-txt-record">Step 4: Configure DMARC</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-to-fix-sendgrid-dns-verification-failed-errors-4-common-provider-gotchas">Fix DNS verification failed errors</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="sendgrid-email-authentication-specification-automated-cnames-vs-manual-txt-records">SendGrid Email Authentication Specification (Automated CNAMEs vs Manual TXT Records)</h2>
        <p>
          SendGrid Domain Authentication (formerly called Whitelabel) <strong class="text-ink">replaces the default <code>sendgrid.net</code> return-path address and shared signing domain with your own custom domain across 3 DNS layers: a branded bounce subdomain (<code>emXXXX</code>) for SPF alignment, dual <code>s1._domainkey</code> and <code>s2._domainkey</code> selectors for 2048-bit DKIM signing, and a root <code>_dmarc</code> TXT policy</strong>. SendGrid enables <strong class="text-ink">Automated Security</strong> by default, which uses 3 <code>CNAME</code> records so SendGrid manages SPF IP updates and DKIM key rotation automatically without consuming any of your root domain's 10 SPF lookups. The table below details every DNS record used in a production SendGrid authentication setup:
        </p>

        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
          <table class="pdns-table w-full text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Protocol / Function</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Type</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Host / Name Field</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Points To / Value</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">SPF Lookup Cost</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">1. Automated Return-Path (SPF Alignment)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">CNAME</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">em1234</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">0 lookups on root @ (Evaluated on em1234 subdomain)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">2. Automated DKIM Key #1 (Primary)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">CNAME</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">s1._domainkey</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">s1.domainkey.u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">0 lookups</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">3. Automated DKIM Key #2 (Rotation)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">CNAME</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">s2._domainkey</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">s2.domainkey.u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">0 lookups</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">4. Root SPF Include (Manual Security / Fallback)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">TXT</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">@</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">v=spf1 include:sendgrid.net ~all</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-amber-400">+2 total DNS lookups (1 top-level + 1 nested ab.sendgrid.net)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">5. DMARC Policy Record</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">TXT</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">_dmarc</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:dmarc@example.com;</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">0 lookups</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">6. Optional Link Branding (Click Tracking)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">CNAME (x2)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">url1234 and 1234567</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">sendgrid.net</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">0 lookups</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          Choosing between Automated Security (ON) and Manual Security (OFF) dictates your operational maintenance burden. Automated Security requires publishing 3 CNAME records (<code>emXXXX</code>, <code>s1._domainkey</code>, and <code>s2._domainkey</code>), allowing SendGrid to rotate 2048-bit keys and update sending IP pools without customer intervention. Turning Automated Security OFF forces administrators to publish raw MX and TXT records (using the legacy selector <code>smtpapi._domainkey</code> and requiring <code>include:sendgrid.net</code> in root SPF), creating manual maintenance overhead outlined in our guide on <a href="/email-authentication/">Email Authentication</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="step-1-how-to-start-domain-authentication-in-the-sendgrid-dashboard-4-steps">Step 1: How to Start Domain Authentication in the SendGrid Dashboard (4 Steps)</h2>
        <p>
          Generate your custom DNS records inside Twilio SendGrid by completing <strong class="text-ink">4 configuration steps in the Sender Authentication wizard</strong>:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Navigate to Sender Authentication:</strong> Open your SendGrid dashboard, select <strong class="text-ink">Settings</strong> from the left sidebar, click <strong class="text-ink">Sender Authentication</strong>, and choose <strong class="text-ink">Authenticate Your Domain</strong> under Domain Authentication.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Select DNS Host and Link Branding Preferences:</strong> Pick your DNS provider from the dropdown menu (such as Cloudflare, Amazon Route 53, GoDaddy, or Namecheap, or select <strong class="text-ink">I'm not sure / Other Host</strong>) and leave <strong class="text-ink">Brand the links for this domain</strong> set to <strong class="text-ink">No</strong> unless configuring dedicated click-tracking hostnames.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Enter the Sending Domain and Review Advanced Settings:</strong> Enter the primary domain used in your sender From address (such as <code>example.com</code>), expand <strong class="text-ink">Advanced Settings</strong>, and ensure the checkbox for <strong class="text-ink">Use automated security</strong> remains checked.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Assign Custom Selectors and Generate Records:</strong> Enter a custom 3-character prefix (such as <code>sgd</code> or <code>mta</code>) under <strong class="text-ink">Custom DKIM Selector</strong> if existing services occupy the default <code>s1</code> and <code>s2</code> names, then click <strong class="text-ink">Next</strong> to display your unique CNAME records and DMARC TXT record.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mt-4">
          Custom Return-Path configuration in Advanced Settings provides branding consistency. SendGrid assigns an automated random prefix like <code>em1234.example.com</code> for envelope bounce handling by default; checking <strong class="text-ink">Use a custom return path</strong> enables specifying an administrative label such as <code>bounces.example.com</code> while achieving full <a href="/dmarc-alignment/">DMARC Alignment</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="step-2-how-to-set-up-sendgrid-spf-emxxxx-cname-vs-includesendgridnet">Step 2: How to Set Up SendGrid SPF (emXXXX CNAME vs include:sendgrid.net)</h2>
        <p>
          Configuring <a href="/spf-record/">SPF Record</a> authentication for SendGrid involves <strong class="text-ink">2 distinct approaches depending on whether you deploy SendGrid's Automated Security emXXXX CNAME or merge include:sendgrid.net directly into your root domain's TXT record</strong>:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Configuration A: Automated Security SPF via the emXXXX CNAME (Recommended):</strong> Publish the first CNAME record generated by SendGrid (<code>Host: em1234</code>, <code>Value: u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net</code>). SendGrid sets the envelope Return-Path bounce address (<code>RFC 5321.MailFrom</code>) to <code>bounces@em1234.example.com</code> when transmitting an outbound email. Receiving mail servers evaluate SPF against <code>em1234.example.com</code>, which resolves directly to SendGrid's managed SPF record. This passes SPF authentication and relaxed DMARC alignment (<code>aspf=r</code>) without consuming any of your root domain's 10 DNS lookups.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Configuration B: Adding include:sendgrid.net to Your Root Domain SPF Record:</strong> Organizations operating with Automated Security turned OFF (or requiring root domain authorization for SendGrid servers) add <code>include:sendgrid.net</code> into their existing root SPF string:
            <div class="my-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <pre><code># Standalone SendGrid root SPF record:
v=spf1 include:sendgrid.net ~all

# Combined with Google Workspace and Microsoft 365 (1 record total):
v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com include:spf.protection.outlook.com include:sendgrid.net ~all</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mt-4">
          Publishing multiple <code>v=spf1</code> records on the root domain invalidates SPF validation completely. Consolidating all authorized sending services into a single TXT entry preserves deliverability, while adding <code>include:sendgrid.net</code> consumes +2 DNS lookups toward <a href="/spf-lookup-limit/">The SPF 10-Lookup Limit Explained</a>. Administrators verify record syntax using the <a href="/spf-record-checker/">SPF Record Checker</a> or reduce nested lookups via the <a href="/spf-flattener/">SPF Flattener</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="step-3-how-to-set-up-sendgrid-dkim-s1domainkey-and-s2domainkey-cnames">Step 3: How to Set Up SendGrid DKIM (s1._domainkey and s2._domainkey CNAMEs)</h2>
        <p>
          Enable 2048-bit <a href="/dkim/">DKIM Explained</a> cryptographic signatures in SendGrid by publishing <strong class="text-ink">both delegated _domainkey CNAME records (s1._domainkey and s2._domainkey) in your authoritative DNS zone across 3 operational steps</strong>:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Copy DKIM CNAME Hostnames and Target Values:</strong> Retrieve the two DKIM CNAME records displayed in your SendGrid Domain Authentication screen:
            <div class="my-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <pre><code>Type: CNAME | Host: s1._domainkey | Value: s1.domainkey.u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net
Type: CNAME | Host: s2._domainkey | Value: s2.domainkey.u1234567.wl001.sendgrid.net</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Add CNAME Records to Your Authoritative DNS Zone:</strong> Create both CNAME records inside your DNS manager. Enter only <code>s1._domainkey</code> and <code>s2._domainkey</code> in the Host/Name input box on DNS platforms like Cloudflare, GoDaddy, and Namecheap that append your domain name automatically.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Disable HTTP Proxy Features on Mail DNS Records:</strong> Set the proxy status to <strong class="text-ink">DNS Only (Grey Cloud)</strong> in Cloudflare for <code>em1234</code>, <code>s1._domainkey</code>, and <code>s2._domainkey</code> so SendGrid's validation systems and destination mail servers query the actual CNAME targets directly.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mt-4">
          Publishing dual DKIM selectors ensures zero-downtime key rotation. SendGrid signs active messages using <code>s1._domainkey</code> while maintaining <code>s2._domainkey</code> as a standby record, allowing automated 2048-bit key rotation without mail delivery disruption. Domain owners confirm record publication through the <a href="/dkim-record-checker/">DKIM Record Checker</a> and test DNS resolution via the <a href="/cname-record-lookup/">CNAME Record Lookup</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="step-4-how-to-configure-dmarc-for-sendgrid-dmarc-txt-record">Step 4: How to Configure DMARC for SendGrid (_dmarc TXT Record)</h2>
        <p>
          Complete SendGrid authentication and satisfy Gmail, Yahoo, and Microsoft bulk-sender requirements by publishing <strong class="text-ink">a single _dmarc TXT policy record on your root domain across 3 steps</strong>:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Check for Existing DMARC Records:</strong> Confirm whether your domain already publishes a <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a> policy using the <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/">DMARC Record Checker</a>. Domains maintain exactly one <code>_dmarc</code> TXT record that governs Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, and SendGrid simultaneously.
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Publish a Monitoring DMARC Policy with Reporting:</strong> Generate a Stage-1 monitoring policy using the <a href="/dmarc-record-generator/">DMARC Record Generator</a> if your domain lacks an existing record, adding a designated <code>rua=mailto:</code> mailbox to collect aggregate XML reports:
            <div class="my-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <pre><code>Host:  _dmarc
Type:  TXT
Value: v=DMARC1; p=none; sp=none; adkim=r; aspf=r; rua=mailto:dmarc-reports@example.com;</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink">Verify Records Inside SendGrid:</strong> Return to the SendGrid Sender Authentication screen, check <strong class="text-ink">I've added these records</strong>, and click <strong class="text-ink">Verify</strong> to confirm all records show green checkmarks.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mt-4">
          SendGrid achieves dual DMARC alignment through automated records. Because <code>em1234.example.com</code> shares the root organizational domain of <code>example.com</code>, it passes relaxed SPF alignment (<code>aspf=r</code>). Signing headers with <code>d=example.com</code> guarantees relaxed and strict DKIM alignment (<code>adkim=r</code> and <code>adkim=s</code>).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="how-to-fix-sendgrid-dns-verification-failed-errors-4-common-provider-gotchas">How to Fix SendGrid DNS Verification Failed Errors (4 Common Provider Gotchas)</h2>
        <p>
          SendGrid's Verify button fails when <strong class="text-ink">your DNS provider alters CNAME targets with an HTTP proxy, doubles domain suffixes in Host fields, or restricts underscore characters in record names</strong>. The table below lists the 4 most common SendGrid verification errors and how to resolve each one across major DNS platforms:
        </p>

        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
          <table class="pdns-table w-full text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Verification Error Symptom</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Root Cause in DNS Control Panel</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Exact Resolution</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cloudflare: All 3 CNAMEs fail to verify</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm text-ink-dim">Cloudflare Proxy status (Orange Cloud) is active on emXXXX, s1._domainkey, or s2._domainkey.</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">Edit all 3 CNAME records in Cloudflare and toggle proxy status to DNS Only (Grey Cloud).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">GoDaddy / Namecheap: Record not found</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm text-ink-dim">Entering s1._domainkey.example.com in the Host field created s1._domainkey.example.com.example.com.</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">Remove the .example.com suffix so the Host field contains only s1._domainkey, s2._domainkey, and em1234.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">s1._domainkey conflicts with existing service</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm text-ink-dim">Another email platform or secondary SendGrid subuser already occupies s1._domainkey.</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">Delete the pending authentication, restart the wizard, open Advanced Settings, and enter a unique Custom DKIM Selector (such as sg2).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Legacy DNS registrar rejects underscore (_)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm text-ink-dim">Older DNS systems block underscore characters in CNAME hostnames while allowing them on TXT records.</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-sm font-medium text-accent">Restart the SendGrid wizard with Automated Security unchecked (OFF) to publish raw TXT keys instead of CNAME records.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          DNS providers such as GoDaddy and Amazon Route 53 require up to 15 minutes to synchronize records across authoritative nameservers, making a brief pause before clicking Verify helpful for avoiding false negative results. Finalizing your SendGrid SPF, DKIM, and DMARC setup through Automated Security establishes verifiable sender reputation. Deploying the emXXXX return-path CNAME alongside dual s1._domainkey and s2._domainkey records eliminates manual key rotation, while maintaining a single _dmarc TXT policy guarantees total DMARC compliance across global receiving inboxes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
              What is the SendGrid SPF record include value?
              <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              The official SendGrid SPF mechanism is <strong class="text-ink">include:sendgrid.net</strong> (for example, <strong class="text-ink">v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com include:sendgrid.net ~all</strong>). Enabling SendGrid Domain Authentication with Automated Security (the emXXXX CNAME) authenticates SPF on that subdomain automatically without requiring include:sendgrid.net on your root domain.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
              Why does SendGrid give me CNAME records instead of TXT records for SPF and DKIM?
              <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              By default, SendGrid enables <strong class="text-ink">Automated Security</strong>, which uses <strong class="text-ink">3 CNAME records</strong> (emXXXX, s1._domainkey, and s2._domainkey) pointing to SendGrid's zone. This delegates SPF IP management and 2048-bit DKIM key rotation to SendGrid so your authentication never breaks during infrastructure updates.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
              Is adding include:sendgrid.net to my root SPF record necessary if I set up the 3 SendGrid CNAMEs?
              <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              In practice, root SPF records close to the 10-DNS-lookup limit omit include:sendgrid.net and rely on the <strong class="text-ink">emXXXX CNAME</strong> (which costs 0 lookups on your root domain and passes DMARC alignment). Add include:sendgrid.net to your root SPF only if you disabled Automated Security.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
              Why is SendGrid's default v=DMARC1; p=none; record missing a rua= tag?
              <span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              In real-world setups, SendGrid generates a bare <strong class="text-ink">v=DMARC1; p=none;</strong> snippet simply to satisfy Gmail and Yahoo's minimum requirement without knowing where to send your reports. Always append <strong class="text-ink">rua=mailto:your-report-address@yourdomain.com;</strong> to receive daily XML visibility into who sends as your domain.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
